```tsx
const sorted = useMemo(() => [...rows].sort(byName), [rows])
const onSelect = useCallback((id: string) => {
  setSelected(id)
}, [])

const Child = memo(function Child({ onSelect }: { onSelect: (id: string) => void }) {
  renderCount++
  return <button onClick={() => onSelect('a')}>pick</button>
})
// cheap: skip useCallback — native button, identity is not a contract
<button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>open</button>
// React Compiler (opt-in, React 19) may already memo sorted / onSelect
```

`sorted` stays the same array when `rows` is unchanged, so a memo child does not re-render. `onSelect` with `[]` is stable and stale if it later reads a changing filter — that is the hurt case.

