// reactive(map) would break instanceof and proxy the whole library
```

Swap the page in one assignment. Patch in place only with `triggerRef`. `markRaw` keeps Mapbox as itself.

example.vi:
```ts
const rows = shallowRef<Row[]>([])
function setPage(next: Row[]) {
  rows.value = next // một lần trigger
}
function patchRow(i: number, row: Row) {
  rows.value[i] = row
  triggerRef(rows) // bắt buộc: ghi tại chỗ vô hình
}
const el = document.getElementById('map')!
const map = markRaw(new MapboxMap({ container: el }))
// reactive(map) sẽ gãy instanceof và proxy cả thư viện
```

Swap trang trong một assignment. Sửa tại chỗ chỉ với `triggerRef`. `markRaw` giữ Mapbox là chính nó.

example.en:
Requirements: an internal admin grid of about [N] rows, filter, sort, row select, and an export. Users do not need every row painted. Components: a cursor API, a cheap row renderer, TanStack Vue Virtual only if the product insists on a long scroll, plus an unvirtualized CSV export so find-in-page and print still work. Data flow: server filter/sort; the client holds a window of rows and selected ids. Trade-off: virtualization recovers INP and empties find-in-page — ship the dump. I would not v-for 50k nodes. Sketch:

```ts
const virtualizer = useVirtualizer({
  count: rows.length,
  getScrollElement: () => parent.value,
  estimateSize: () => 36,
  overscan: 8,
})
// Render virtualizer.getVirtualItems() only.
// Selection is a Set of ids, never indexes.
```

Happy to go into the search-abort story if they want an incident instead of this design.

